# Shell scripts must keep LF on every platform. With core.autocrlf=true and no
# .gitattributes, a Windows checkout rewrites them to CRLF, and a CRLF bash script
# fails on its shebang ("bad interpreter: /usr/bin/env bash^M").
*.sh text eol=lf

# This file pins itself for the same reason: a CRLF here would attach a stray
# carriage return to the last token and corrupt the eol value below/above.
.gitattributes text eol=lf

# NORMALISE EVERY TEXT FILE TO LF IN THE REPOSITORY, so a Windows checkout and a
# Linux container build cannot disagree about line endings.
#
# WHY THIS WAS ADDED. Commit f51a3b9 reported "75 files changed, 23821 insertions,
# 23791 deletions" for what was a two-file, 34-line fix. Ignoring CR-at-EOL the
# same commit was exactly 2 files / 34 lines; the other 73 were core.autocrlf=true
# rewriting every CRLF it staged. The code was unharmed, but real changes were
# buried in thousands of phantom lines - and the one-line defect that commit fixed
# was itself nearly lost in that noise.
#
# eol=lf IS THE POINT, not text=auto alone: it pins the working tree too, so the
# next Windows edit does not reintroduce CRLF and start the churn again. The *.sh
# rule above is now redundant but kept, because it documents the concrete failure
# that motivated it.
* text=auto eol=lf

# Binary: text normalisation would CORRUPT these. auto usually detects them, but an
# explicit list is cheap and this is not a place to rely on a heuristic.
*.png binary
*.jpg binary
*.jpeg binary
*.gif binary
*.webp binary
*.ico binary
*.pdf binary
*.zip binary
*.woff binary
*.woff2 binary
*.ttf binary
*.xlsx binary
*.docx binary
